  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem. The only difference between this and the hard version is the constraint on NNN:\n\nhere, the sum of NNN across all tests won't exceed 200020002000.**\n\nYou are given a permutation†^\\dagger† PPP of the integers 111 to NNN.\n\n**At most once**, you can do the following operation:\n\n\nChoose two indices iii and jjj (1≤i&lt;j≤N1 \\leq i \\lt j \\leq N1≤i&lt;j≤N) and swap PiP_iPi​ and PjP_jPj​.\nFind the maximum possible number of increasing subarrays that PPP can contain after this operation.\n\nThe subarray of PPP from index LLL to index RRR is said to be increasing if\n\nPL&lt;PL+1&lt;…&lt;PRP_L \\lt P_{L+1} \\lt \\ldots \\lt P_RPL​&lt;PL+1​&lt;…&lt;PR​†^\\dagger† A permutation of the integers 111 to NNN is an array of length NNN that contains every integer from 111 to NNN.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the size of the permutation.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the maximum possible number of increasing subarrays after at most one swap.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤20001 \\leq N \\leq 20001≤N≤2000",
      "1≤Pi≤N1 \\leq P_i \\leq N1≤Pi​≤N",
      "Pi≠PjP_i \\neq P_jPi​=Pj​ for i≠ji \\neq ji=j",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 200020002000."
    ],
